From bf07041e04a0707824222cab1245b5d9f015393c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kyle Mylonakis <kyle@protopia.ai>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 14:05:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Bound the as_strided extent guard by the allocation, not
 the layout

The extent check this PR added to the as_strided lowering compared the
requested extent against `old_layout.storage_size()`, the buffer's logical
layout. That is not what a buffer holds. `inplace_constant_pad_nd()`
deliberately allocates a buffer larger than its layout so a later pad can be
written in place, records the enlarged size in `V.graph.buffer_to_padded_size`,
and codegen sizes the allocation through `V.graph.get_allocation_storage_size()`.
Bounding by the layout therefore rejected that intentional over-allocation as if
it were a view reaching past its buffer, breaking `test_linear_and_cel`,
`test_pad_non_zero_memory_leak` and `test_donated_buffer_inplace_gpt` with
errors of the form "requires 51511256 elements but buf1 holds only 51511249",
where the difference is exactly the pad width.

The guard now takes its bound from `get_allocation_storage_size()`, which is the
same number codegen allocates by, so a deliberate over-allocation is accepted
while a genuinely undersized buffer is still rejected. The registration in
`buffer_to_padded_size` happens before the `as_strided` call it feeds, so the
padded size is already visible when the guard runs. The alternative was to
special-case buffers appearing in `buffer_to_padded_size`; asking the allocation
directly was preferred because it is the single source of truth codegen already
uses and stays correct for any future pass that inflates an allocation.

Two comments were overstated and are corrected here. A realized buffer is not
"allocated to exactly its own layout" -- that is the assumption this fix
removes. And an unprovable symbolic extent does not "fail loudly": `check_leq`
raises only when the shape env can refute the inequality, and otherwise defers a
runtime assertion.

The new tests cover two review questions. The inference-mode test answers
whether `tensor._base` survives `torch.inference_mode()`, since if it did not,
the mutated-view clone would fall back to spanning the base extent of a buffer
sized to the view; it does survive, because AOTAutograd re-enables view tracking
for tracing, and forcing `base = None` makes the test fail. The two unbacked
tests pin what the deferred half of the guard actually does: an over-extent that
can only be refuted at runtime is caught on a cold cache but not on a warm FX
graph cache, because a cache hit replays the artifact without re-running this
lowering, so the deferred assertion is never registered. They disable caches to
pin the covered half, and the comment now states the uncovered half rather than
implying the guard is total.

Review `torch/_inductor/lowering.py` first, then the tests.

Test Plan:

The three tests that the layout-bound guard broke, each confirmed failing before
this change and passing after:

```
python test/inductor/test_inplace_padding.py
python test/inductor/test_torchinductor.py -k test_donated_buffer_inplace_gpt
python test/inductor/test_aot_inductor.py -k test_pad_non_zero_memory_leak
```

The extent-check and mutated-view regressions, on CPU and CUDA:

```
python test/inductor/test_torchinductor.py -k as_strided
python test/inductor/test_triton_kernels.py -k mutated
```

Results: inplace padding 9/9, donated buffer 1/1, AOTI pad 2/2 (2 skipped for
MPS/CPU-only), as_strided 20/20, mutated 7/7. Lint via `spin lint` and
`lintrunner -a` is clean. Run on an NVIDIA GB10 (sm_121, aarch64).

This change was authored with the assistance of an AI coding agent (Claude).

