From 2c01073d8f0f121c10c5c06166157602ce4e2575 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kyle Mylonakis <kyle@protopia.ai>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 14:18:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Cover the column-slice-of-an-intermediate geometry from
 #161115

The existing regressions here slice rows out of a freshly created base with a
strided `::gap` view. The shape that reaches this bug from real workloads looks
different: a contiguous column prefix of an intermediate, non-dense because the
row stride is much larger than the slice, at a zero storage_offset. That is what
the minimal repro for #161115 does, and it exercises a different realization path
-- the base is a computed buffer rather than an input -- so the existing
parametrization did not reach it.

n_rows is load-bearing and the value is not arbitrary. Measured against the
pre-fix code with this same kernel and slicing: at 8 rows Inductor keeps the view
inside the workspace buffer, so there is no undersized allocation and nothing to
detect; at 64 rows the clone is filled from past the end of the buffer and 3584
of 32768 elements come back wrong; at 512 rows the read leaves the allocation
entirely and raises a CUDA illegal memory access, which would poison the context
for every test that followed it in the same process. 64 is the smallest of those
that reproduces the corruption without faulting.

This also confirms the fix covers #161115. Reverting the four torch/ files this
PR touches to the merge base makes the new test fail, and restoring them makes it
pass; the issue's own minimal repro behaves the same way, miscompiling 2092990 of
2097152 elements before the fix and passing three cold-cache runs after it. The
manifestation differs from the issue report -- it was filed as a hard CUDA
illegal memory access and reproduces here as silent NaNs -- because whether an
out-of-bounds read faults depends on whether the address happens to be mapped.
No `Fixes` keyword is claimed: what was verified is the minimal repro rather than
the original vLLM workload, so closing that issue is left to its assignee.

Test Plan:

```
python test/inductor/test_triton_kernels.py -k mutated
```

