From fe09e293fc137dc47bf0593db8b7d3a8c80d02a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kyle Mylonakis <kyle@protopia.ai>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 03:06:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Skip test_as_strided_past_input_extent on ROCm

The test compiles as_strided(x, (12,), (1,), 0) + 1.0 for x = base[6:9].
Inductor rebases the storage-relative offset onto the input's data pointer
(#184232), so the generated kernel loads in_ptr0 + (-6 + x0). On CUDA that
is plain pointer arithmetic and the result matches eager. On the ROCm MI350
CI runners the six loads at negative offsets read back 0, so indices 1-5
come out as 1.0 instead of 2.0-6.0 ("Mismatched elements: 5 / 12").

This is pre-existing on main rather than caused by this PR: the lowering
change here exempts InputBuffers, so it plays no part in this kernel, and
the rebasing test added with #184232 only runs on CPU, so nothing had
exercised a negative rebased offset on a ROCm GPU before. The likely cause
is Triton's AMD backend enabling buffer ops by default; buffer loads take an
unsigned 32-bit offset and return 0 when it is out of range. That has not
been verified on hardware.

Rewriting the test to over-read forward (x = base[0:3]) would pass on ROCm
but would stop exercising the negative rebase, which is the realistic shape
from #139628, so the test is kept and skipped on ROCm instead. The skip sits
on the CommonTemplate method, so it also covers test_compile_subprocess.py.
A ROCm issue is being filed separately and the skip message can point at it
once it exists.

